.TH CHTTPSVR_CREATE_MP 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_create_mp \- create an HTTP server with a custom allocator
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

chttpsvr chttpsvr_create_mp(ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mprocs,
                            clog cl,
                            char **err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Allocates and initializes a new chttpsvr handle with the memory
management procedures you give it, and creates a root router with an
empty prefix.

mprocs may be NULL, and the function then uses the default system
allocator.

cl decides the logger of the server, and it may be CLOG_INVALID:

- cl == CLOG_INVALID: the server creates its own internal logger,
  which writes only FATAL messages to stderr.
- cl != CLOG_INVALID: the server derives a new logger from cl (see
  clog_derive) and tags it with the field component=http-server.
  The server uses and manages the derived logger, while the caller's
  cl handle doesn't change and stays the caller's.  When cl is
  closed, or was never a valid handle, the function fails: it sets
  err_str to "the parent logger is not open" and returns
  CHTTPSVR_INVALID.  It does not end the process, which clog_derive
  does for such a handle.

Either way, internal or derived, the server closes its own logger
when the server is destroyed.

On failure the function returns CHTTPSVR_INVALID and, when err_str
is not NULL, sets err_str to a static diagnostic string.

chttpsvr is an opaque value handle (a uint64_t) and not a pointer.
Before the library uses the server, it resolves the handle through a
table of its own and checks a generation.  Do not cast the handle to
or from a pointer.  Compare it with CHTTPSVR_INVALID, which is 0, so
a truth test such as if (!srv) works too.  A call with the handle of
a destroyed server is refused, and fatal where chttpsvr_destroy(3)
says so, but it never leads to a use-after-free.

You can run more than one process that serves traffic with this
library in two supported ways.  The first is to fork(2) before the
process creates and starts any server, so that each process, parent
and child alike, calls chttpsvr_create_mp and chttpsvr_start by
itself and builds its own server from scratch.  The second is a
fork(2) followed at once by an exec(3), which is what popen, system
and launching a subprocess from a request handler do.  That is fully
supported whether or not a server runs at that moment, because the
program that exec(3) starts replaces the process image completely
and never touches what this library left behind.  A process that
keeps running with no exec(3) in between, while a server it created
before the fork is still live, is not a supported pattern.

On top of that the library has one defense-in-depth measure.  A new
call to chttpsvr_create_mp, chttpsvr_start, chttpsvr_stop,
chttpsvr_destroy, chttpsvr_register_handler,
chttpsvr_register_streaming_handler, chttpsvr_use or
chttpsvr_subrouter never hangs on a lock that another thread held at
the instant of the fork, a thread that may not exist any more
because fork(2) duplicates only the calling thread.  This holds for a
call from any thread, in the parent and in a newly forked child that
has not called exec(3).  It also covers a fork in the middle of a
start, a stop or a destroy of that exact handle, and a fork in the
middle of a shutdown of the whole shared engine driven by
chttpsvr_engine_stop().

That measure does not change anything for a server that is live and
running work when such a fork happens, and such a server is
explicitly out of scope.  Its reactor threads, worker-pool threads
and idle-timeout-sweep threads are not carried over into the child,
so a started server that the child inherits can't accept new
connections or process in-flight work there.  chttpsvr_stop()
followed by chttpsvr_start() on that handle in the child doesn't
bring it back either: the call reports ccol_success, but the server
still cannot serve, because it reuses the engine reference it held
before the fork instead of building a new one.  A caller that wants
none of this protection can compile it out with
-DCCOL_FORK_SAFETY_REQUIRED=0.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A valid chttpsvr handle on success, or CHTTPSVR_INVALID on failure.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
At exit, the library's destructor releases its table of server
handles once no server is left.  A call made after that point, for
example from a later destructor or atexit handler of the
application, returns CHTTPSVR_INVALID with an error string, and every
other function treats any handle it receives as invalid.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_create (3),
.BR chttpsvr_start (3),
.BR clog_derive (3)
